## 6. Severity

| Level | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|
| `BLOCKER` | Breaks the hard invariant, a layer direction, a context boundary, or is a security defect | Earliest refactor branch |
| `MAJOR` | Structural gap that will produce runtime defects | Scheduled branch |
| `MINOR` | Naming, placement, ceremony | One batched cleanup branch |
| `INFO` | Observation or deliberate trade-off | No action |

---

## 7. Known reference deviations — judge, do not copy blindly

The reference is the target, but three of its choices are debatable. Report them as `INFO` with the trade-off named; do not silently propagate them.

1. **`User` exposes `@Setter` on every field.** The assembler needs them, but so does anyone else, so the aggregate cannot fully protect its invariants. Alternative: a reconstruction constructor or a static factory used only by the assembler.
2. **`UserPersistenceEntity` uses `FetchType.EAGER` on `@ManyToMany`.** It avoids `LazyInitializationException` at the cost of loading roles on every user query, including queries that never read them.
3. **`Result` / `ApplicationError` coexists with exception-based rollback.** Mixing the two means some failures roll back and some commit. Pick one per operation and record which.

